{"type":"turn_start"}
{"type":"message_start","message":{"role":"system","content":"","sections":{"preamble":"You are a coding assistant running inside pi, a coding agent harness. Help users inspect files, run commands, and modify code.","tools":"<tools>\n- read: Read file contents\n- bash: Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)\n- edit: Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including multiple disjoint edits in one call\n- write: Create or overwrite files\n- visualize: Draw a bar or line chart (Unicode in the result, SVG file in the artifacts folder)\n- Task: Launch subagents (foreground, background with completion notifications, resume by agent ID)\n- stop_agent: Stop a running subagent by agent ID\n- agent_status: Check subagents read-only (running or idle, last response) without restarting them\n- subscribe_github_pr: Wake this session when a GitHub PR changes\n- subscribe_timer: Schedule a prompt with cron or a delay\n- subscribe_webhook: Create a webhook routine that wakes this session\n- subscribe_slack: Wake this session (or start a fresh run) when a Slack channel gets a message\n- list_subscriptions: List subscriptions\n- unsubscribe: End a subscription\n- bash_background: Run a shell command in the background, with notifications when it finishes or prints a watched line\n- AwaitShell: Poll or wait for a background shell job, or sleep\n- TodoWrite: Create and update the todo list for this session\n- AskQuestion: Structured multiple-choice questions for the user\n- request_secret: Ask the user for a secret through a masked field; the value goes to a credential file, never to you\n\nAdditional tools may be available.\n</tools>","rules":"<rules>\n- Use bash for file operations like ls, rg, find\n- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.\n- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.\n- Use edit for targeted changes. Batch changes to one file in a single call.\n- Each edits[].oldText must exactly and uniquely match the original file. Keep matches short and non-overlapping; merge nearby changes without including large unchanged regions.\n- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.\n- When a skill or playbook says to open a todolist or todo list, call TodoWrite before other work, with the steps it names as the first items.\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>","docs":"<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about the harness itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: /Users/josh-desktop/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.5.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/README.md\n- Additional docs: /Users/josh-desktop/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.5.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs\n- Examples: /Users/josh-desktop/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.5.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md), MCP servers (docs/mcp.md), codemode scripts and non-LLM models such as classifiers and image models (docs/codemode.md)\n- Before implementing harness changes, read the relevant docs and examples in full, following related cross-references.\n</docs>","skills":"<skills>\nThe following skills provide specialized instructions for specific tasks.\nUse the read tool to load a skill's file when the task matches its description.\nResolve relative paths against the skill's directory (parent of SKILL.md). Use absolute paths in tool calls.\n\n<available_skills>\n  <skill>\n    <name>check-compiler-errors</name>\n    <description>Run compile and type-check commands and report failures</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/check-compiler-errors/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>control-cli</name>\n    <description>Build or adapt a local harness to drive, inspect, and profile an interactive CLI or TUI without external services. Use for CLI UX checks, startup regressions, memory leaks, hangs, prompt flows, or terminal demos.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/control-cli/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>control-ui</name>\n    <description>Build or adapt a local browser/CDP harness to drive and inspect a web, IDE, or Electron UI. Use for local UI verification, screenshots, accessibility snapshots, perf profiles, visual diffs, or reproducing UI bugs.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/control-ui/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>create-skill</name>\n    <description>Create Pi Agent Skills. Use when authoring a new skill or asking about SKILL.md structure.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/create-skill/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>deslop</name>\n    <description>Remove AI-generated code slop and clean up code style</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/deslop/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>fix-ci</name>\n    <description>Find failing PR checks, inspect logs or external check links, and apply focused fixes</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/fix-ci/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>fix-merge-conflicts</name>\n    <description>Resolve merge conflicts non-interactively, validate build and tests, and finalize conflict resolution</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/fix-merge-conflicts/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>get-pr-comments</name>\n    <description>Fetch and summarize review comments from the active pull request</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/get-pr-comments/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>loop-on-ci</name>\n    <description>Monitor PR checks and fix failures until green. Uses gh pr checks as the source of truth for PR-attached checks.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/loop-on-ci/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>make-pr-easy-to-review</name>\n    <description>Prepare PRs for review by cleaning noisy history, improving PR descriptions, and adding reviewer guidance without changing code behavior. Use for &quot;make this easy to review&quot;, &quot;tidy this PR&quot;, &quot;clean up commits&quot;, or &quot;annotate the diff&quot;.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/make-pr-easy-to-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>new-branch-and-pr</name>\n    <description>Create a fresh branch, complete work, and open a pull request</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/new-branch-and-pr/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>review-and-ship</name>\n    <description>Review the current branch for bugs, intent fit, and test coverage; run or write tests; commit focused work; open or update a PR.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/review-and-ship/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>run-smoke-tests</name>\n    <description>Run Playwright smoke tests, debug failures, and verify fixes</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/run-smoke-tests/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>setup-pstack</name>\n    <description>Configure which models pstack uses per role and at what reasoning budget. Detects your available models and writes the model rule, an always-applied rule that overrides the skill defaults. Use for /setup-pstack, &quot;configure pstack models&quot;, &quot;pstack budget&quot;, or changing pstack&apos;s model choices.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/setup-pstack/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>verify-this</name>\n    <description>Verify a claim with fresh local evidence: restate it falsifiably, capture baseline and treatment, compare artifacts, and return VERIFIED, NOT VERIFIED, or INCONCLUSIVE.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/verify-this/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>weekly-review</name>\n    <description>Produce a weekly synthesis of authored commits with highlights by bugfix, tech debt, and net-new work</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/weekly-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>what-did-i-get-done</name>\n    <description>Summarize authored commits over a user-specified time period into a concise update</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/what-did-i-get-done/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>workflow-from-chats</name>\n    <description>Extract durable working preferences from recent Pi chats and convert them into skills, rules, or workflow docs. Use when asked to learn preferences, mine feedback, personalize workflows, or generate team/person-specific agent guidance.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/workflow-from-chats/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>\n</skills>","cwd":"<cwd>\n/private/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/work\n</cwd>","pstack_host":"<pstack_host>\npstack runs here through the pi-pstack package at /private/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack. Today's date: Saturday Nov 7, 2026.\n- <SKILLS> = /private/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills. \"the X skill\", \"/X\", and \"principle-X\" mean <SKILLS>/X/SKILL.md, even for skills hidden from automatic invocation; a delegate that needs poteto-mode reads <SKILLS>/poteto-mode/SKILL.md. Installed skills (78): architect, arena, automate, automate-me, benchmark-checklist, blast-radius, bro, check-compiler-errors, control-cli, control-ui, correct, create-formal-verification-skill, create-skill, create-verification-skill, deslop, figure-it-out, fix-ci, fix-merge-conflicts, get-pr-comments, how, in-your-own-words, interrogate, ketchup, ketchup-video, loop-on-ci, maintain-formal-verification-skill, maintain-verification-skill, make-bot-ui, make-pr-easy-to-review, new-branch-and-pr, no-comments, poteto-help, poteto-mode, pr-review-canvas, principle-attack-the-premise, principle-boundary-discipline, principle-build-the-lever, principle-encode-lessons-in-structure, principle-exhaust-the-design-space, principle-experience-first, principle-explain-the-number, principle-fix-root-causes, principle-foundational-thinking, principle-guard-the-context-window, principle-laziness-protocol, principle-make-operations-idempotent, principle-migrate-callers-then-delete-legacy-apis, principle-minimize-reader-load, principle-model-the-domain, principle-never-block-on-the-human, principle-outcome-oriented-execution, principle-prove-it-works, principle-redesign-from-first-principles, principle-separate-before-serializing-shared-state, principle-sequence-verifiable-units, principle-subtract-before-you-add, principle-test-behavior-not-implementation, principle-type-system-discipline, recall, reflect, review-and-ship, run-smoke-tests, setup-pstack, sharpen, show-me-your-work, statechart, swarm, tdd, teach, technical-writing, thermo-nuclear-code-quality-review, typescript-best-practices, unslop, verify-this, weekly-review, what-did-i-get-done, why, workflow-from-chats.\n- A <skill> block in a user message means the user attached that skill to this request. Follow its workflow from its first step, including the steps it says to take before answering, instead of answering directly.\n- Skill folders: project `.pi/skills/`, personal `/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/skills/`.\n- The model rule is /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/pstack/pstack-models.md.\n- Subagents: call the Task tool as the skills describe (description, prompt, subagent_type, model, readonly, run_in_background, resume, fork, fork_turns, environment, cloud_base_branch, file_attachments). Types and model slugs are in <pstack_subagents>. A background agent notifies you after your turn ends; do not poll it.\n- Todo lists: the TodoWrite tool. Structured questions: the AskQuestion tool, but nobody can answer in this run, so every question comes back skipped; decide yourself and say what you chose.\n- Transcripts: this workspace's Pi session files are in /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/sessions. Each line is an entry: the first is a session header, and conversation turns have type \"message\" (the opening prompt is the first user message). This chat's transcript is /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/sessions/2026-11-07T16-50-16-055Z_dec54bd0-e03c-4f4a-8d98-14b516bdb2a3.jsonl. Subagent transcripts for this workspace: /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/pstack/workspaces/--private-var-folders-11-m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn-T-pstack-corpus-ur5cbW-21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2-pi-work--/subagents/*_<agent id>.jsonl. A subagent is still running while /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/pstack/workspaces/--private-var-folders-11-m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn-T-pstack-corpus-ur5cbW-21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2-pi-work--/subagents/<agent id>.lock exists and names a live pid.\n- In a Pi transcript a tool call is a toolCall part of an assistant message (tool name plus arguments), and an attached skill is a <skill name=\"...\" location=\"...\"> block in a user message.\n- \"The current agent's store\" is /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/pstack/workspaces/--private-var-folders-11-m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn-T-pstack-corpus-ur5cbW-21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2-pi-work--/store/dec54bd0-e03c-4f4a-8d98-14b516bdb2a3. Create it when a playbook needs it. \"The root agent's store\" is /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/pstack/workspaces/--private-var-folders-11-m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn-T-pstack-corpus-ur5cbW-21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2-pi-work--/store/dec54bd0-e03c-4f4a-8d98-14b516bdb2a3, the store of the main chat that started this subagent tree; in the main chat it is the current agent's store.\n- Projects: a program's Project is /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/pstack/workspaces/--private-var-folders-11-m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn-T-pstack-corpus-ur5cbW-21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2-pi-work--/projects/<project-slug>/, with notes.md as its Project tracker and store/ as its Project store. Every agent in this workspace sees the same folder.\n- Files a tool makes for the user, such as visualize charts, go in /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/pstack/workspaces/--private-var-folders-11-m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn-T-pstack-corpus-ur5cbW-21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2-pi-work--/artifacts/dec54bd0-e03c-4f4a-8d98-14b516bdb2a3.\n- Subscriptions: this run ends when it settles, so nothing can wake it and the subscribe tools refuse. Name in the final reply any PR, timer, or webhook the parent chat should subscribe to.\n- agent_status reads a subagent's state without waking it. Task resume sends a follow-up to an agent that has finished, and fails while the agent still runs. Task fork starts a new agent from a copy of an idle agent's transcript (fork_turns keeps only its first N user turns), in that agent's directory. stop_agent stops a running agent.\n- /loop: this run ends when it settles (a subagent, or a print or JSON run), so it has no Loop tool and cannot arm a loop. Keep working until the goal is met, and name in the final reply anything the parent chat should loop on.\n- Background shells: bash_background runs a command in the background, or moves it there after block_until_ms, and writes its output to /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/pstack/workspaces/--private-var-folders-11-m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn-T-pstack-corpus-ur5cbW-21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2-pi-work--/terminals/<shell id>.txt. The first lines of that file hold pid, cwd, command, title, status, and running_for_ms (`head -n 10 *.txt` there lists every job), and a footer with exit_code appears when the command ends. A notification wakes you after your turn when a job finishes and on each notify_on_output match; AwaitShell polls or waits. Stop a job with `kill <pid>`. bash waits for its command up to its timeout, 30 seconds when the call sets none, and then moves a command still running to the background, so start anything that can run for minutes (a watcher, a polling script such as watch-pr without --status-only, a dev server) with bash_background.\n- Image generation: no image model is configured, so this session has no GenerateImage tool. Say so and use a mermaid diagram or a table instead.\n- The user keeps a skill on every turn with `/mode <skill>` (exit `/mode off`) and plans without edits with `/plan` (exit `/plan off`).\n- PR operations use the forge command the step gives, such as `gh pr create`. Bugbot comments on a GitHub PR are ordinary review threads; skip a step that starts, waits for, or configures Bugbot when the PR has no Bugbot check or comment.\n- MCP tools are named mcp__<server>__<tool>; `pi mcp list` shows the servers.\n- Media in a reply: Pi's chat renders no image or video inside a reply. To show a screenshot or a frame, read the image file with the read tool, which displays it inline in terminals that support images, and name its path. For a watchable catch-up video, open the local file with the system player (`open` on macOS, `xdg-open` on Linux), read one frame from each slide, and name the video path. Pi chat still does not play the video inline.\n- pi-pstack's README (/private/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/README.md) and guide (/private/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/docs/guide) describe installing and using it.\n- Cloud agents (Task environment \"cloud\") run as detached workers on this host, not on their own machine, because no container engine answers `docker version`: each in its own git worktree, created from cloud_base_branch or this checkout's HEAD commit, so uncommitted changes do not reach it. They share this host's ports, credentials, simulators, and files, so give concurrent workers distinct ports, output directories, and writable cache paths. Merge the branch to take a worker's work. Pi never deletes these worktrees or branches; the worktree-cleanup playbook prunes them. Each starts on branch pstack/<agent id>. It keeps running when this chat closes; its result arrives here once the chat is open again. agent_status shows its machine and run; stop_agent stops it. A repository describes its cloud machine (image or build.dockerfile, install, start, terminals) in .pi/environment.json.\n</pstack_host>","pstack_subagents":"<pstack_subagents>\nAvailable subagent_types and a quick description of what they do:\n- generalPurpose: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. Use when searching for a keyword or file and not confident you'll find the match quickly.\n- explore: Fast agent specialized for exploring codebases. Use this when you need to quickly find files by patterns (eg. \"src/components/**/*.tsx\"), search code for keywords (eg. \"API endpoints\"), or answer questions about the codebase (eg. \"how do API endpoints work?\"). When calling this agent, specify the desired thoroughness level: \"quick\" for basic searches, \"medium\" for moderate exploration, or \"very thorough\" for comprehensive analysis across multiple locations and naming conventions.\n- shell: Command execution specialist for running bash commands. Use this for git operations, command execution, and other terminal tasks.\n- ci-watcher: Watch PR CI for the current branch and report pass/fail with relevant failure links. Use when waiting for CI results or CI has failed. Use proactively to monitor branch CI.\n- comment-sicko: A deranged comment-hater that savors deletion and condemns workaround code.\n- poteto-agent: Delegate subagent for code-writing and helper tasks spawned during a `/poteto-mode` run. Use for delegates, not as the runner for `/poteto-mode` itself. Spawn a fresh `poteto-agent` for each new task, and resume one only in the strict cases that poteto-mode's Subagents section names. Reads the `poteto-mode` skill's `SKILL.md` in full before any work, including its inline Principles index. Substituting `generalPurpose` skips that read and drifts.\n- thermo-nuclear-code-quality-review: Thermo-nuclear code quality audit (maintainability, structure, 1k-line rule, spaghetti, code-judo). Invoked via Task after a parent gathers diff and file contents. Loads the rubric from the `thermo-nuclear-code-quality-review` skill shipped with pi-pstack.\nModel values for Task (and for role lines in the model rule):\n- inherit (default): the parent model and thinking level, except that explore runs on the parent model's -fast variant when one is listed. auto and inherit-parent mean the same.\n- This chat runs openai/gpt-5.6-sol:medium. pstack's model families (claude-*, grok-*) go by the model id after its provider.\n- A Pi model <provider>/<model-id> or <model-id>, optionally with :<level> from the thinking levels the model lists below. A level the model does not list is rejected.\n- Model parameters: <model>[effort=<level>,fast=true|false]. effort is the thinking level, fast=true picks the model's -fast id when one is listed, and [] or fast=false keeps the standard model.\nAvailable models: deepseek/deepseek-flash (thinking: off, low, high, max), deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro (thinking: off, high, max), openai/chat-latest (thinking: off, minimal, low, medium, high), openai/gpt-4, openai/gpt-4-turbo, openai/gpt-4.1, openai/gpt-4.1-mini, openai/gpt-4.1-nano, openai/gpt-4o, openai/gpt-4o-2024-05-13, openai/gpt-4o-2024-08-06, openai/gpt-4o-2024-11-20, openai/gpt-4o-mini, openai/gpt-5 (thinking: minimal, low, medium, high), openai/gpt-5-chat-latest, openai/gpt-5-mini (thinking: minimal, low, medium, high), openai/gpt-5-nano (thinking: minimal, low, medium, high), openai/gpt-5-pro (thinking: high), openai/gpt-5.1 (thinking: off, low, medium, high), openai/gpt-5.2 (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.2-pro (thinking: medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.3-codex (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.3-codex-spark (thinking: low, medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.4 (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.4-mini (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.4-nano (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.4-pro (thinking: medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.5 (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.5-pro (thinking: medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.6-cyber (thinking: low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-5.6-luna (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-5.6-sol (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-5.6-terra (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-6-astra (thinking: low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-6-luna (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-6-sol (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-6.1-sol (thinking: low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-6.1-terra (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-daybreak-blue-latest (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-daybreak-red-latest (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/o1 (thinking: low, medium, high), openai/o1-pro (thinking: low, medium, high), openai/o3 (thinking: low, medium, high), openai/o3-mini (thinking: low, medium, high), openai/o3-pro (thinking: low, medium, high), openai/o4-mini (thinking: low, medium, high)\nUse inherit unless the user or a skill asks for another model. When the user names a model family without a version, use the newest listed version of that family.\nIf the user asks for a model that is not listed, do not substitute another model or guess: skip launching that subagent and tell the user which model was unavailable and which models are available.\nWhen you tell the user which model a subagent used, name it the way the user named it, not by its model ID, unless the user used the ID.\n</pstack_subagents>"},"timestamp":1794070217667,"toolsAdded":[{"name":"read","description":"Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"},"offset":{"type":"number","description":"Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of lines to read"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"bash","description":"Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds. The working directory and exported environment variables carry over to the next call in this session. A command that runs exit, times out, or is aborted keeps neither. A command still running when its timeout elapses is not stopped: it moves to the background, and the result names its shell ID, PID, and output file. Without a timeout, that happens after 30 seconds. AwaitShell waits on it, and a notification arrives after your turn when it finishes.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["command"],"properties":{"command":{"type":"string","description":"Shell command to execute"},"timeout":{"type":"number","description":"Seconds to wait for the command before it moves to the background, where it keeps running (default 30)"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"edit","description":"Edit a single file using exact text replacement. Every edits[].oldText must match a unique, non-overlapping region of the original file. If two changes affect the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead of emitting overlapping edits. Do not include large unchanged regions just to connect distant changes.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","edits"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to edit (relative or absolute)"},"edits":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["oldText","newText"],"properties":{"oldText":{"type":"string","description":"Exact text for one targeted replacement. It must be unique in the original file and must not overlap with any other edits[].oldText in the same call."},"newText":{"type":"string","description":"Replacement text for this targeted edit."}}},"description":"One or more targeted replacements. Each edit is matched against the original file, not incrementally. Do not include overlapping or nested edits. If two changes touch the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead."}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"write","description":"Write content to a file. Creates the file if it doesn't exist, overwrites if it does. Automatically creates parent directories.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","content"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to write (relative or absolute)"},"content":{"type":"string","description":"Content to write to the file"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"visualize","description":"Draw a bar or line chart from labels and one or more named series. The result shows the chart as Unicode text and names an SVG file written to the session artifacts folder.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["title","kind","labels","series"],"properties":{"title":{"type":"string","description":"Chart title."},"kind":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","const":"bar"},{"type":"string","const":"line"}],"description":"bar: one bar per label and series. line: one line per series across the labels."},"labels":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Category or x-axis labels, at most 500."},"series":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["name","values"],"properties":{"name":{"type":"string"},"values":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"number"},"description":"One value per label."}}},"description":"One or more named series, at most 32."},"unit":{"type":"string","description":"Unit shown after values, such as ms or %."}}}},{"name":"Task","description":"Launch a new agent to handle complex, multi-step tasks autonomously.\n\nThe Task tool launches specialized subagents (subprocesses) that autonomously handle complex tasks. Each subagent_type has specific capabilities and tools available to it.\n\nWhen using the Task tool, you must specify a subagent_type parameter to select which agent type to use.\n\nIf the query is a narrow or specific question, you should NOT use the Task and instead address the query directly using the other tools available to you.\n\nExamples:\n- user: \"Where is the ClientError class defined?\" assistant: [Uses grep directly - this is a needle query for a specific class]\n- user: \"Run this query using my database API\" assistant: [Calls the MCP directly - this is not a broad exploration task]\n- user: \"What is the codebase structure?\" assistant: [Uses the Task tool with subagent_type=\"explore\"]\n\nIf it is possible to explore different areas of the codebase in parallel, you should launch multiple agents concurrently.\n\nWhen NOT to use the Task tool:\n- Simple, single or few-step tasks that can be performed by a single agent (using parallel or sequential tool calls) -- just call the tools directly instead.\n- For example:\n  - If you want to read a specific file path, use the read or find tool instead of the Task tool, to find the match more quickly\n  - If you are searching for code within a specific file or set of 2-3 files, use the read tool instead of the Task tool, to find the match more quickly\n  - If you are searching for a specific class definition like \"class Foo\", use the find tool instead, to find the match more quickly\n\nUsage notes:\n- Always include a short description (3-5 words) summarizing what the agent will do\n- Launch multiple agents concurrently whenever possible, to maximize performance; to do that, use a single message with multiple tool uses.\n- When the agent is done, it will return a single message back to you. Specify exactly what information the agent should return back in its final response to you.\n- Agents can be resumed using the `resume` parameter by passing the agent ID from a previous invocation. This sends a follow-up message after the agent has completed, preserving existing context. If the agent is still running, the request fails; wait for completion before resuming. When NOT resuming, each invocation starts fresh and you should provide a detailed task description with all necessary context.\n- When using the Task tool, the subagent invocation does not have access to the user's message or prior assistant steps. Therefore, you should provide a highly detailed task description with all necessary context for the agent to perform its task autonomously.\n- The subagent's outputs should generally be trusted\n- Clearly tell the subagent which tasks you want it to perform, since it is not aware of the user's intent or your prior assistant steps (tool calls, thinking, or messages).\n- If the subagent description mentions that it should be used proactively, then you should try your best to use it without the user having to ask for it first. Use your judgement.\n- If the user specifies that they want you to run subagents \"in parallel\", you MUST send a single message with multiple Task tool use content blocks. For example, if you need to launch both a code-reviewer subagent and a test-runner subagent in parallel, send a single message with both tool calls.\n- Avoid delegating the full query to the Task tool and returning the result. In these cases, you should address the query using the other tools available to you.\n\nAvailable subagent_types and descriptions are listed in <pstack_subagents> in the system prompt.\n\nAvailable model slugs for subagents are listed in <pstack_subagents> in the system prompt.\n\nWhen an agent runs in the background, you will be automatically notified when it completes after you end your own turn - do NOT AwaitShell, poll, or proactively check on its progress. Continue with other work or end your turn instead. Don't mention this to the user.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["description","prompt"],"properties":{"description":{"type":"string","description":"A short, user-friendly title for the subagent. This appears in the UI as the subagent's name. Make it concrete and distinct, consider recent titles to avoid reuse. For resumed subagents which you are prompting to work on a separate task, give an updated description based on the latest work the subagent is performing. (Do not rename if the subagent is continuing work on the same high-level task.)"},"prompt":{"type":"string","description":"The task for the agent to perform"},"subagent_type":{"type":"string","description":"Subagent type to use for this task. Available types are listed in <pstack_subagents> in the system prompt."},"model":{"type":"string","description":"Optional model slug for this agent. If provided, it must resolve to one of the available model slugs. The available model slugs are listed in <pstack_subagents> in the system prompt. An invalid slug fails the call with the allowed list — pick a listed slug or omit this parameter; never retry an invalid slug unchanged. If omitted, the subagent uses the same model as the parent agent. Do not pass if resume field is set (prior model will be used). Use \"inherit\" unless the user explicitly requested another listed model."},"resume":{"type":"string","description":"Optional agent ID to resume from. If provided, sends a follow-up message to the agent after it has completed. Requests to a currently running asynchronous agent fail; wait for completion before resuming."},"fork":{"type":"string","description":"Agent ID from an earlier Task call in this workspace. Starts a new agent (new ID) whose context begins as a copy of that agent transcript and that runs in the same place (its cloud machine when it had one), so it sees that checkout and its uncommitted changes. The prompt is the new task. subagent_type, model, and readonly default to the source agent; readonly can be added, never lifted. Fails while the source is running. Cannot be combined with resume, environment, or cloud_base_branch."},"fork_turns":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"description":"With fork: copy only the first N user turns of the source agent, so the fork starts from that point in its conversation. Defaults to the whole transcript."},"run_in_background":{"type":"boolean","description":"Run the agent in the background (returns output_file path to check later). If this is false, you will be blocked until the agent completes. When true, the background subagent will send a notification when it completes."},"readonly":{"type":"boolean","description":"Limit the agent to read, grep, find, ls, and a read-only shell that cannot write files (network reads work, state-changing commands are not allowed), with no edits and no MCP tools. A readonly subagent type stays readonly."},"file_attachments":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Optional array of file paths to images or videos to attach to the subagent. Files are read and attached to the subagent's context. Use to forward relevant media to any subagent (e.g. pass a user-attached image's saved file path so the subagent sees the actual image rather than a prose description)."},"environment":{"enum":["local","cloud"],"type":"string","description":"local (default) runs the agent in this working directory. cloud runs it as a cloud agent on its own machine (a container with its own filesystem, processes, and ports) with a fresh clone of the pushed remote on a new branch pstack/<agent id>, from cloud_base_branch, else the current branch when it is pushed, else the default branch. Local uncommitted or unpushed changes, transcripts, simulators, and credentials do not reach it. It keeps running when this chat closes, and its result arrives here when the chat is back. The agent pushes its branch; take its commits from there. Ignored with resume; not allowed with fork."},"cloud_base_branch":{"type":"string","description":"With environment cloud, the pushed branch the cloud agent starts from. Defaults to the current branch when the remote has it, else the default branch. Needs environment cloud."}}}},{"name":"stop_agent","description":"Stop a running subagent: abort its run and end its process. Its result (the completion notification of a background agent, or the waiting Task result of a foreground one) reports the stop. An agent that is not running is left alone. Resume a stopped agent with Task resume.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["agent_id"],"properties":{"agent_id":{"type":"string","description":"Agent ID from an earlier Task call in this workspace."}}}},{"name":"agent_status","description":"Read-only status of subagents in this workspace: running or idle, type, model, directory, a cloud agent's machine and branch, created and updated times, and the last response. This is the way to check on a worker; it never starts, messages, or resumes anything. Task resume would message a running agent or restart an idle one instead.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"agent_id":{"type":"string","description":"Agent ID from an earlier Task call in this workspace. Omit it to list the most recent agents, newest first."}}}},{"name":"subscribe_github_pr","description":"Watch GitHub pull requests with gh and wake this session when one changes: state (merged, closed, reopened), head SHA, mergeability, review decision, new reviews or comments, or a check concluding. The first poll only records a baseline. Polls every minute until unsubscribed or expired.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["scope"],"properties":{"scope":{"enum":["pr","repo","author"],"type":"string","description":"pr: one pull request (prUrl). repo: every open PR in repo, including PRs opened, closed, or merged. author: open PRs in repo by author."},"prUrl":{"type":"string","description":"scope pr: the pull request URL, such as https://github.com/owner/repo/pull/123."},"repo":{"type":"string","description":"scope repo or author: owner/name."},"author":{"type":"string","description":"scope author: a GitHub login, or @me."},"name":{"type":"string","description":"A name to manage the subscription by; defaults to the target."},"expiresInHours":{"type":"number","description":"Hours until the subscription ends; default 168 (7 days), at most 720."}}}},{"name":"subscribe_timer","description":"Fire a prompt on a 5-field cron schedule (local time) or after delaySeconds (repeating unless once). wake_self queues the prompt for this owning session, including while offline. new_session uses a background Task while the owner is live, or a detached run with recorded status while offline. Re-subscribing an existing name returns created: false and changes nothing.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["name","prompt"],"properties":{"name":{"type":"string","description":"Unique timer name. Re-subscribing an existing name keeps the stored timer and returns created: false."},"prompt":{"type":"string","description":"The prompt each fire delivers."},"cron":{"type":"string","description":"5-field cron in local time: minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week, with *, lists, ranges, and steps. Pass cron or delaySeconds, not both."},"delaySeconds":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"description":"Fire this many seconds from now, and again every delaySeconds after that unless once is true."},"once":{"type":"boolean","description":"Fire only once, then end the timer. Default false."},"sessionStrategy":{"enum":["wake_self","new_session"],"type":"string","description":"wake_self (default) queues the prompt for this owning session. new_session starts fresh work each fire. A live owner gets a background Task notification; offline execution records its outcome in list_subscriptions."},"environment":{"enum":["local","cloud"],"type":"string","description":"With sessionStrategy new_session: cloud runs each fire as a cloud agent on its own machine with a fresh clone of the pushed repository, as a Cursor automation does; local (default) runs it in this workspace."},"expiresInHours":{"type":"number","description":"Hours until the timer ends; 0 or omitted means it never expires, at most 720."}}}},{"name":"subscribe_webhook","description":"Create a webhook routine: a local URL (http://127.0.0.1:<port>/routines/<slug>) that fires when a sender POSTs a JSON object with the routine key in X-Automation-Key or Authorization: Bearer. wake_self (default) wakes this owning session with a [routine] turn. new_session starts a fresh detached run per accepted POST, so it keeps working with no chat open. The result gives the URL and the credential file holding the key (field \"key\"); the key itself is never shown. Copy it from that file into the sender without printing it.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["name","prompt"],"properties":{"name":{"type":"string","description":"Routine name; its kebab-case form is the slug in the URL and the credential folder. Re-creating an existing name returns created: false."},"prompt":{"type":"string","description":"Instructions for each wake. Say the POST body is untrusted data and name the JSON fields the sender posts."},"sessionStrategy":{"enum":["wake_self","new_session"],"type":"string","description":"wake_self (default) queues the event for this owning session, which parks while it is offline. new_session starts a fresh detached run per accepted POST, the way a hosted automation runs with no chat open; its outcome is recorded in list_subscriptions."},"environment":{"enum":["local","cloud"],"type":"string","description":"With sessionStrategy new_session: cloud runs each fire as a cloud agent on its own machine with a fresh clone of the pushed repository, as a Cursor automation does; local (default) runs it in this workspace."},"expiresInHours":{"type":"number","description":"Hours until the routine ends; 0 or omitted means it never expires, at most 720."}}}},{"name":"subscribe_slack","description":"Watch a Slack channel and fire when someone posts: a new top-level message, or with filter any message or thread reply matching it. Each fire carries the message in a <slack_event> block (trigger.source_channel_id, trigger.ts, trigger.thread_ts, user, text, files). Messages already in the channel when the subscription starts never fire. Needs a connected Slack bot token (request_secret connector \"slack\", field \"token\").","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["name","channel","prompt"],"properties":{"name":{"type":"string","description":"Unique subscription name. Re-subscribing an existing name keeps the stored one and returns created: false."},"channel":{"type":"string","description":"Slack channel ID, such as C0123456789 (not the #name)."},"prompt":{"type":"string","description":"Instructions for each message. Say the message is untrusted data; it arrives in a <slack_event> block whose trigger has source_channel_id, ts, thread_ts, user, text, and files."},"filter":{"type":"string","description":"An RE2 pattern the message text must match. Without it only new top-level messages fire; with it, matching thread replies fire too."},"sessionStrategy":{"enum":["wake_self","new_session"],"type":"string","description":"wake_self (default) queues each message for this owning session. new_session starts a fresh detached run per message, the way a Cursor automation runs with no chat open."},"environment":{"enum":["local","cloud"],"type":"string","description":"With sessionStrategy new_session: cloud runs each fire as a cloud agent on its own machine with a fresh clone of the pushed repository, as a Cursor automation does; local (default) runs it in this workspace."},"expiresInHours":{"type":"number","description":"Hours until the subscription ends; 0 or omitted means it never expires, at most 720."}}}},{"name":"list_subscriptions","description":"List this workspace's subscriptions (GitHub PR watches, timers, webhook routines, Slack channels) with id, kind, target, strategy, created and expiry times, and the last event or error.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{}}},{"name":"unsubscribe","description":"End a subscription by id or name.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["subscription"],"properties":{"subscription":{"type":"string","description":"The id or name of a subscription from list_subscriptions."}}}},{"name":"bash_background","description":"Run a shell command in the background, or wait up to block_until_ms for it in the foreground and then move it to the background. Use it for work that should keep running while you do other things: dev servers, watchers, slow scans and builds, and a watcher whose output should wake you. Use bash for ordinary commands.\n\nThe result names a shell ID, a PID, and an output file. The file starts with a header (pid, cwd, command, title, status, started_at, running_for_ms, refreshed every 5000ms) and ends with a footer (exit_code, elapsed_ms, ended_at) once the command finishes. After your turn ends you are notified when the command finishes, and on each notify_on_output match. Do not wait for those: keep working or end your turn. Poll with AwaitShell only when you are blocked on this job. Stop a job with `kill <pid>`.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["command"],"properties":{"command":{"type":"string","description":"The command to execute"},"description":{"type":"string","description":"Very short phrase naming what this command does; it titles the notifications about it. Avoid leading with verbs or using trailing periods"},"block_until_ms":{"type":"number","description":"How long to wait for the command in the foreground before moving it to the background, in milliseconds. 0 (the default) starts it in the background at once. A command that finishes within the wait returns its output and sends no notification."},"notify_on_output":{"type":"object","required":["pattern","reason"],"properties":{"pattern":{"type":"string","description":"Regex pattern matched against each line of stdout/stderr output. Output redirected only to a file will not trigger it. Do not match all outputs."},"reason":{"type":"string","description":"5 or less words describing why you are watching for this output. It titles the notification."},"debounce_ms":{"type":"number","description":"Milliseconds that must elapse between notifications. The minimum is 5000ms."}},"description":"Optional output notification config. Each output line which matches the pattern will notify you. ONLY set this when the user explicitly requests monitoring."},"working_directory":{"type":"string","description":"The absolute path to the working directory to execute the command in (defaults to current directory)"}}}},{"name":"AwaitShell","description":"Check or poll a backgrounded shell job. For work that does not have a shell id, you can omit the shell_id arg to sleep for the full `block_until_ms` duration (prefer this over sleeping in the shell). At the end of your turn, you will be notified about any unawaited jobs that completed. If you think a job completed (e.g. because you killed it), observe it with AwaitShell to skip the notification, because stale notifications can confuse the user.\n\nPrefer NOT to poll reflexively with AwaitShell. Multitask on independent work while backgrounded jobs run, or finish your turn and rely on the end-of-turn completion notification. Poll with AwaitShell only when one of the following is true:\n- Your very next step is blocked on this specific job's result and you have no other productive work to do, OR\n- The task requires close monitoring (see shell guidance below).\n- NEVER USE THIS TO POLL OR WAIT VACUOUSLY FOR A SUBAGENT LAUNCHED WITH THE Task TOOL — rely on the end-of-turn completion notification instead (it is delivered as soon as the subagent finishes; guessing a wait time is inefficient).\n- Shell: only poll with AwaitShell when the command requires close monitoring. Close monitoring means a long-running job that can silently hang, degrade, or need a course correction before it completes — e.g. training runs, eval runs, deployments, long builds, datagen pipelines, DB migrations, large data transfers. For fire-and-forget commands (tests, installs, dev servers/watchers, short scripts, etc.) the completion notification is enough — start them, keep working, and only poll with AwaitShell later if you end up blocked on the result.\n- Shell sanity check (regardless of close monitoring): when you spawn a command directly into the background (`block_until_ms: 0`), do a single status check by reading the output file to confirm the command didn't fail to start. This is a one-shot smoke check, not a polling loop.\n- Shell close-monitoring guidance (only applies in the close-monitoring case above):\n  - HARD STOPPING CONSTRAINT: once you've decided to actively poll, don't stop until (a) the job terminates, (b) the command reaches a healthy steady state (only for non-terminating commands, e.g. dev server/watcher), or (c) the command is hung — follow the hang guidance below.\n  - Waiting until a regex matches the output can be useful for e.g. known startup/status/error logs.\n  - Size `block_until_ms` to the command's expected runtime. When waiting further, avoid round 5-minute waits: prefer slices of 60–270s (keeps prompt cache warm) or 1200s+ (one cache miss buys a long wait).\n  - Output file header has `pid` and `running_for_ms` (updated every 5000ms).\n  - When finished, footer with `exit_code` and `elapsed_ms` appears (regex only matches the body, not header/footer).\n  - If the command is taking longer than expected and appears hung (use judgment based on command type), kill the process if safe to do so using the pid in the header. If possible, fix the hang and proceed.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"shell_id":{"type":"string","description":"Optional shell id to poll. If omitted, this tool sleeps for the full block_until_ms duration and then returns. Required when block_until_ms is 0."},"block_until_ms":{"type":"number","maximum":7140000,"description":"Max sleep time to block before returning (in milliseconds). Defaults to 30000ms. Set to 0 only together with shell_id, for a non-blocking status check of that shell. Must not exceed 7140000 (119 minutes)."},"pattern":{"type":"string","description":"Block until the regex matches stdout/stderr stream (or task completes). Matches anywhere in the shell output, not just new output. Will not match terminal file headers or footers, e.g. exit_code. Accepts RE2 regex syntax (no lookahead, lookbehind or backreferences), compiled in multiline mode so ^ and $ match at line boundaries."},"waiting_for_subagent":{"type":"boolean","description":"Set this to true if you are waiting for subagent(s) to complete. Remember you should NOT be doing this and instead end your turn or do parallel work."}}}},{"name":"TodoWrite","description":"Use this tool to create and manage a structured task list for your current coding session. This helps track progress, organize complex tasks, and demonstrate thoroughness.\n\nNote: Other than when first creating todos, don't tell the user you're updating todos, just do it.\n\n### When to Use This Tool\n\nUse proactively for:\n1. Complex multi-step tasks (3+ distinct steps)\n2. Non-trivial tasks requiring careful planning\n3. User explicitly requests todo list\n4. User provides multiple tasks (numbered/comma-separated)\n5. After receiving new instructions - capture requirements as todos (use merge=false to add new ones)\n6. After completing tasks - mark complete with merge=true and add follow-ups\n7. When starting new tasks - mark as in_progress (ideally only one at a time)\n\n### When NOT to Use\n\nSkip for:\n1. Single, straightforward tasks\n2. Trivial tasks with no organizational benefit\n3. Tasks completable in < 3 trivial steps\n4. Purely conversational/informational requests\n5. Don't add a task to test the change unless asked, or you'll overfocus on testing\n\n### Examples\n\n<example>\n  User: Add dark mode toggle to settings\n  Assistant:\n    - *Creates todo list:*\n      1. Add state management [in_progress]\n      2. Implement styles\n      3. Create toggle component\n      4. Update components\n    - [Immediately begins working on todo 1 in the same tool call batch]\n<reasoning>\n  Multi-step feature with dependencies.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n<example>\n  User: Rename getCwd to getCurrentWorkingDirectory across my project\n  Assistant: *Searches codebase, finds 15 instances across 8 files*\n  *Creates todo list with specific items for each file that needs updating*\n\n<reasoning>\n  Complex refactoring requiring systematic tracking across multiple files.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n<example>\n  User: Implement user registration, product catalog, shopping cart, checkout flow.\n  Assistant: *Creates todo list breaking down each feature into specific tasks*\n\n<reasoning>\n  Multiple complex features provided as list requiring organized task management.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n<example>\n  User: Optimize my React app - it's rendering slowly.\n  Assistant: *Analyzes codebase, identifies issues*\n  *Creates todo list: 1) Memoization, 2) Virtualization, 3) Image optimization, 4) Fix state loops, 5) Code splitting*\n\n<reasoning>\n  Performance optimization requires multiple steps across different components.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n### Examples of When NOT to Use the Todo List\n\n<example>\n  User: What does git status do?\n  Assistant: Shows current state of working directory and staging area...\n\n<reasoning>\n  Informational request with no coding task to complete.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n<example>\n  User: Add comment to calculateTotal function.\n  Assistant: *Uses edit tool to add comment*\n\n<reasoning>\n  Single straightforward task in one location.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n<example>\n  User: Run npm install for me.\n  Assistant: *Executes npm install* Command completed successfully...\n\n<reasoning>\n  Single command execution with immediate results.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n### Task States and Management\n\n1. **Task States:**\n  - pending: Not yet started\n  - in_progress: Currently working on\n  - completed: Finished successfully\n  - cancelled: No longer needed\n\n2. **Task Management:**\n  - Update status in real-time\n  - Mark complete IMMEDIATELY after finishing\n  - Only ONE task in_progress at a time\n  - Complete current tasks before starting new ones\n\n3. **Task Breakdown:**\n  - Create specific, actionable items\n  - Break complex tasks into manageable steps\n  - Use clear, descriptive names\n\n4. **Parallel Todo Writes:**\n  - Prefer creating the first todo as in_progress\n  - Start working on todos by using tool calls in the same tool call batch as the todo write\n  - Batch todo updates with other tool calls for better latency and lower costs for the user\n\nWhen in doubt, use this tool. Proactive task management demonstrates attentiveness and ensures complete requirements.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["todos","merge"],"properties":{"todos":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["id"],"properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"Unique identifier for the TODO item"},"content":{"type":"string","description":"The description/content of the TODO item. Required for a new item; may be omitted when merging an update."},"status":{"enum":["pending","in_progress","completed","cancelled"],"type":"string","description":"The current status of the TODO item. Required for a new item; may be omitted when merging an update."}}},"minItems":1,"description":"Array of TODO items to update or create"},"merge":{"type":"boolean","description":"Whether to merge the todos with the existing todos. If true, the todos will be merged into the existing todos based on the id field. You can leave unchanged properties undefined. If false, the new todos will replace the existing todos."}}}},{"name":"AskQuestion","description":"Collect structured multiple-choice answers from the user. Use this tool only when you are blocked on a decision that is genuinely the user's to make: one you cannot resolve from the request, the code, or sensible defaults.\n\nUsage notes:\n- Each question should have at least 2 options for the user to choose from\n- Users will always be able to select \"Other\" to provide custom text input\n- Use allow_multiple: true to allow multiple answers to be selected for a question\n- If you recommend a specific option, make that the first option in the list and add \"(Recommended)\" at the end of the label\n\nPrefer this tool over listing options in your final response text (as letters, numbers, bullet points, etc).","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["questions"],"properties":{"title":{"type":"string","description":"Optional title for the questions form"},"questions":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["id","prompt","options"],"properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"Unique identifier for this question"},"prompt":{"type":"string","description":"The question text to display to the user, without the options."},"options":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["id","label"],"properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"Unique identifier for this option"},"label":{"type":"string","description":"Display text for this option"}}},"minItems":2,"description":"Array of answer options (minimum 2 required)"},"allow_multiple":{"type":"boolean","description":"If true, user can select multiple options. Defaults to false."}}},"minItems":1,"description":"Array of questions to present to the user (minimum 1 required)"}}}},{"name":"request_secret","description":"Ask the user for a secret (an API key, a token, a sender key) without it entering the conversation. The user types it into a masked field, and the value is stored in the named connector's credential file under the given field. You never see the value: read it from that file with a command that does not print it, such as jq into a config file. Never ask for a secret in chat.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["label","connector","field"],"properties":{"label":{"type":"string","description":"What the user is asked for, such as \"webhook sender key\" or \"Slack bot token\"."},"connector":{"type":"string","description":"The connector whose credential file stores the value: lowercase letters, digits, and hyphens, such as \"slack\" or a routine slug."},"field":{"type":"string","description":"The field of that credential file, such as \"token\" or \"key\"."}}}}]}}
{"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"system","content":"","sections":{"preamble":"You are a coding assistant running inside pi, a coding agent harness. Help users inspect files, run commands, and modify code.","tools":"<tools>\n- read: Read file contents\n- bash: Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)\n- edit: Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including multiple disjoint edits in one call\n- write: Create or overwrite files\n- visualize: Draw a bar or line chart (Unicode in the result, SVG file in the artifacts folder)\n- Task: Launch subagents (foreground, background with completion notifications, resume by agent ID)\n- stop_agent: Stop a running subagent by agent ID\n- agent_status: Check subagents read-only (running or idle, last response) without restarting them\n- subscribe_github_pr: Wake this session when a GitHub PR changes\n- subscribe_timer: Schedule a prompt with cron or a delay\n- subscribe_webhook: Create a webhook routine that wakes this session\n- subscribe_slack: Wake this session (or start a fresh run) when a Slack channel gets a message\n- list_subscriptions: List subscriptions\n- unsubscribe: End a subscription\n- bash_background: Run a shell command in the background, with notifications when it finishes or prints a watched line\n- AwaitShell: Poll or wait for a background shell job, or sleep\n- TodoWrite: Create and update the todo list for this session\n- AskQuestion: Structured multiple-choice questions for the user\n- request_secret: Ask the user for a secret through a masked field; the value goes to a credential file, never to you\n\nAdditional tools may be available.\n</tools>","rules":"<rules>\n- Use bash for file operations like ls, rg, find\n- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.\n- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.\n- Use edit for targeted changes. Batch changes to one file in a single call.\n- Each edits[].oldText must exactly and uniquely match the original file. Keep matches short and non-overlapping; merge nearby changes without including large unchanged regions.\n- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.\n- When a skill or playbook says to open a todolist or todo list, call TodoWrite before other work, with the steps it names as the first items.\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>","docs":"<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about the harness itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: /Users/josh-desktop/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.5.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/README.md\n- Additional docs: /Users/josh-desktop/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.5.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs\n- Examples: /Users/josh-desktop/.pi/agent/install/releases/1.5.0/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md), MCP servers (docs/mcp.md), codemode scripts and non-LLM models such as classifiers and image models (docs/codemode.md)\n- Before implementing harness changes, read the relevant docs and examples in full, following related cross-references.\n</docs>","skills":"<skills>\nThe following skills provide specialized instructions for specific tasks.\nUse the read tool to load a skill's file when the task matches its description.\nResolve relative paths against the skill's directory (parent of SKILL.md). Use absolute paths in tool calls.\n\n<available_skills>\n  <skill>\n    <name>check-compiler-errors</name>\n    <description>Run compile and type-check commands and report failures</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/check-compiler-errors/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>control-cli</name>\n    <description>Build or adapt a local harness to drive, inspect, and profile an interactive CLI or TUI without external services. Use for CLI UX checks, startup regressions, memory leaks, hangs, prompt flows, or terminal demos.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/control-cli/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>control-ui</name>\n    <description>Build or adapt a local browser/CDP harness to drive and inspect a web, IDE, or Electron UI. Use for local UI verification, screenshots, accessibility snapshots, perf profiles, visual diffs, or reproducing UI bugs.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/control-ui/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>create-skill</name>\n    <description>Create Pi Agent Skills. Use when authoring a new skill or asking about SKILL.md structure.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/create-skill/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>deslop</name>\n    <description>Remove AI-generated code slop and clean up code style</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/deslop/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>fix-ci</name>\n    <description>Find failing PR checks, inspect logs or external check links, and apply focused fixes</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/fix-ci/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>fix-merge-conflicts</name>\n    <description>Resolve merge conflicts non-interactively, validate build and tests, and finalize conflict resolution</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/fix-merge-conflicts/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>get-pr-comments</name>\n    <description>Fetch and summarize review comments from the active pull request</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/get-pr-comments/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>loop-on-ci</name>\n    <description>Monitor PR checks and fix failures until green. Uses gh pr checks as the source of truth for PR-attached checks.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/loop-on-ci/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>make-pr-easy-to-review</name>\n    <description>Prepare PRs for review by cleaning noisy history, improving PR descriptions, and adding reviewer guidance without changing code behavior. Use for &quot;make this easy to review&quot;, &quot;tidy this PR&quot;, &quot;clean up commits&quot;, or &quot;annotate the diff&quot;.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/make-pr-easy-to-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>new-branch-and-pr</name>\n    <description>Create a fresh branch, complete work, and open a pull request</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/new-branch-and-pr/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>review-and-ship</name>\n    <description>Review the current branch for bugs, intent fit, and test coverage; run or write tests; commit focused work; open or update a PR.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/review-and-ship/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>run-smoke-tests</name>\n    <description>Run Playwright smoke tests, debug failures, and verify fixes</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/run-smoke-tests/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>setup-pstack</name>\n    <description>Configure which models pstack uses per role and at what reasoning budget. Detects your available models and writes the model rule, an always-applied rule that overrides the skill defaults. Use for /setup-pstack, &quot;configure pstack models&quot;, &quot;pstack budget&quot;, or changing pstack&apos;s model choices.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/setup-pstack/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>verify-this</name>\n    <description>Verify a claim with fresh local evidence: restate it falsifiably, capture baseline and treatment, compare artifacts, and return VERIFIED, NOT VERIFIED, or INCONCLUSIVE.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/verify-this/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>weekly-review</name>\n    <description>Produce a weekly synthesis of authored commits with highlights by bugfix, tech debt, and net-new work</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/weekly-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>what-did-i-get-done</name>\n    <description>Summarize authored commits over a user-specified time period into a concise update</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/what-did-i-get-done/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>workflow-from-chats</name>\n    <description>Extract durable working preferences from recent Pi chats and convert them into skills, rules, or workflow docs. Use when asked to learn preferences, mine feedback, personalize workflows, or generate team/person-specific agent guidance.</description>\n    <location>/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/workflow-from-chats/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>\n</skills>","cwd":"<cwd>\n/private/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/work\n</cwd>","pstack_host":"<pstack_host>\npstack runs here through the pi-pstack package at /private/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack. Today's date: Saturday Nov 7, 2026.\n- <SKILLS> = /private/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills. \"the X skill\", \"/X\", and \"principle-X\" mean <SKILLS>/X/SKILL.md, even for skills hidden from automatic invocation; a delegate that needs poteto-mode reads <SKILLS>/poteto-mode/SKILL.md. Installed skills (78): architect, arena, automate, automate-me, benchmark-checklist, blast-radius, bro, check-compiler-errors, control-cli, control-ui, correct, create-formal-verification-skill, create-skill, create-verification-skill, deslop, figure-it-out, fix-ci, fix-merge-conflicts, get-pr-comments, how, in-your-own-words, interrogate, ketchup, ketchup-video, loop-on-ci, maintain-formal-verification-skill, maintain-verification-skill, make-bot-ui, make-pr-easy-to-review, new-branch-and-pr, no-comments, poteto-help, poteto-mode, pr-review-canvas, principle-attack-the-premise, principle-boundary-discipline, principle-build-the-lever, principle-encode-lessons-in-structure, principle-exhaust-the-design-space, principle-experience-first, principle-explain-the-number, principle-fix-root-causes, principle-foundational-thinking, principle-guard-the-context-window, principle-laziness-protocol, principle-make-operations-idempotent, principle-migrate-callers-then-delete-legacy-apis, principle-minimize-reader-load, principle-model-the-domain, principle-never-block-on-the-human, principle-outcome-oriented-execution, principle-prove-it-works, principle-redesign-from-first-principles, principle-separate-before-serializing-shared-state, principle-sequence-verifiable-units, principle-subtract-before-you-add, principle-test-behavior-not-implementation, principle-type-system-discipline, recall, reflect, review-and-ship, run-smoke-tests, setup-pstack, sharpen, show-me-your-work, statechart, swarm, tdd, teach, technical-writing, thermo-nuclear-code-quality-review, typescript-best-practices, unslop, verify-this, weekly-review, what-did-i-get-done, why, workflow-from-chats.\n- A <skill> block in a user message means the user attached that skill to this request. Follow its workflow from its first step, including the steps it says to take before answering, instead of answering directly.\n- Skill folders: project `.pi/skills/`, personal `/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/skills/`.\n- The model rule is /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/pstack/pstack-models.md.\n- Subagents: call the Task tool as the skills describe (description, prompt, subagent_type, model, readonly, run_in_background, resume, fork, fork_turns, environment, cloud_base_branch, file_attachments). Types and model slugs are in <pstack_subagents>. A background agent notifies you after your turn ends; do not poll it.\n- Todo lists: the TodoWrite tool. Structured questions: the AskQuestion tool, but nobody can answer in this run, so every question comes back skipped; decide yourself and say what you chose.\n- Transcripts: this workspace's Pi session files are in /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/sessions. Each line is an entry: the first is a session header, and conversation turns have type \"message\" (the opening prompt is the first user message). This chat's transcript is /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/sessions/2026-11-07T16-50-16-055Z_dec54bd0-e03c-4f4a-8d98-14b516bdb2a3.jsonl. Subagent transcripts for this workspace: /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/pstack/workspaces/--private-var-folders-11-m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn-T-pstack-corpus-ur5cbW-21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2-pi-work--/subagents/*_<agent id>.jsonl. A subagent is still running while /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/pstack/workspaces/--private-var-folders-11-m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn-T-pstack-corpus-ur5cbW-21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2-pi-work--/subagents/<agent id>.lock exists and names a live pid.\n- In a Pi transcript a tool call is a toolCall part of an assistant message (tool name plus arguments), and an attached skill is a <skill name=\"...\" location=\"...\"> block in a user message.\n- \"The current agent's store\" is /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/pstack/workspaces/--private-var-folders-11-m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn-T-pstack-corpus-ur5cbW-21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2-pi-work--/store/dec54bd0-e03c-4f4a-8d98-14b516bdb2a3. Create it when a playbook needs it. \"The root agent's store\" is /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/pstack/workspaces/--private-var-folders-11-m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn-T-pstack-corpus-ur5cbW-21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2-pi-work--/store/dec54bd0-e03c-4f4a-8d98-14b516bdb2a3, the store of the main chat that started this subagent tree; in the main chat it is the current agent's store.\n- Projects: a program's Project is /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/pstack/workspaces/--private-var-folders-11-m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn-T-pstack-corpus-ur5cbW-21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2-pi-work--/projects/<project-slug>/, with notes.md as its Project tracker and store/ as its Project store. Every agent in this workspace sees the same folder.\n- Files a tool makes for the user, such as visualize charts, go in /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/pstack/workspaces/--private-var-folders-11-m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn-T-pstack-corpus-ur5cbW-21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2-pi-work--/artifacts/dec54bd0-e03c-4f4a-8d98-14b516bdb2a3.\n- Subscriptions: this run ends when it settles, so nothing can wake it and the subscribe tools refuse. Name in the final reply any PR, timer, or webhook the parent chat should subscribe to.\n- agent_status reads a subagent's state without waking it. Task resume sends a follow-up to an agent that has finished, and fails while the agent still runs. Task fork starts a new agent from a copy of an idle agent's transcript (fork_turns keeps only its first N user turns), in that agent's directory. stop_agent stops a running agent.\n- /loop: this run ends when it settles (a subagent, or a print or JSON run), so it has no Loop tool and cannot arm a loop. Keep working until the goal is met, and name in the final reply anything the parent chat should loop on.\n- Background shells: bash_background runs a command in the background, or moves it there after block_until_ms, and writes its output to /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/pstack/workspaces/--private-var-folders-11-m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn-T-pstack-corpus-ur5cbW-21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2-pi-work--/terminals/<shell id>.txt. The first lines of that file hold pid, cwd, command, title, status, and running_for_ms (`head -n 10 *.txt` there lists every job), and a footer with exit_code appears when the command ends. A notification wakes you after your turn when a job finishes and on each notify_on_output match; AwaitShell polls or waits. Stop a job with `kill <pid>`. bash waits for its command up to its timeout, 30 seconds when the call sets none, and then moves a command still running to the background, so start anything that can run for minutes (a watcher, a polling script such as watch-pr without --status-only, a dev server) with bash_background.\n- Image generation: no image model is configured, so this session has no GenerateImage tool. Say so and use a mermaid diagram or a table instead.\n- The user keeps a skill on every turn with `/mode <skill>` (exit `/mode off`) and plans without edits with `/plan` (exit `/plan off`).\n- PR operations use the forge command the step gives, such as `gh pr create`. Bugbot comments on a GitHub PR are ordinary review threads; skip a step that starts, waits for, or configures Bugbot when the PR has no Bugbot check or comment.\n- MCP tools are named mcp__<server>__<tool>; `pi mcp list` shows the servers.\n- Media in a reply: Pi's chat renders no image or video inside a reply. To show a screenshot or a frame, read the image file with the read tool, which displays it inline in terminals that support images, and name its path. For a watchable catch-up video, open the local file with the system player (`open` on macOS, `xdg-open` on Linux), read one frame from each slide, and name the video path. Pi chat still does not play the video inline.\n- pi-pstack's README (/private/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/README.md) and guide (/private/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/docs/guide) describe installing and using it.\n- Cloud agents (Task environment \"cloud\") run as detached workers on this host, not on their own machine, because no container engine answers `docker version`: each in its own git worktree, created from cloud_base_branch or this checkout's HEAD commit, so uncommitted changes do not reach it. They share this host's ports, credentials, simulators, and files, so give concurrent workers distinct ports, output directories, and writable cache paths. Merge the branch to take a worker's work. Pi never deletes these worktrees or branches; the worktree-cleanup playbook prunes them. Each starts on branch pstack/<agent id>. It keeps running when this chat closes; its result arrives here once the chat is open again. agent_status shows its machine and run; stop_agent stops it. A repository describes its cloud machine (image or build.dockerfile, install, start, terminals) in .pi/environment.json.\n</pstack_host>","pstack_subagents":"<pstack_subagents>\nAvailable subagent_types and a quick description of what they do:\n- generalPurpose: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. Use when searching for a keyword or file and not confident you'll find the match quickly.\n- explore: Fast agent specialized for exploring codebases. Use this when you need to quickly find files by patterns (eg. \"src/components/**/*.tsx\"), search code for keywords (eg. \"API endpoints\"), or answer questions about the codebase (eg. \"how do API endpoints work?\"). When calling this agent, specify the desired thoroughness level: \"quick\" for basic searches, \"medium\" for moderate exploration, or \"very thorough\" for comprehensive analysis across multiple locations and naming conventions.\n- shell: Command execution specialist for running bash commands. Use this for git operations, command execution, and other terminal tasks.\n- ci-watcher: Watch PR CI for the current branch and report pass/fail with relevant failure links. Use when waiting for CI results or CI has failed. Use proactively to monitor branch CI.\n- comment-sicko: A deranged comment-hater that savors deletion and condemns workaround code.\n- poteto-agent: Delegate subagent for code-writing and helper tasks spawned during a `/poteto-mode` run. Use for delegates, not as the runner for `/poteto-mode` itself. Spawn a fresh `poteto-agent` for each new task, and resume one only in the strict cases that poteto-mode's Subagents section names. Reads the `poteto-mode` skill's `SKILL.md` in full before any work, including its inline Principles index. Substituting `generalPurpose` skips that read and drifts.\n- thermo-nuclear-code-quality-review: Thermo-nuclear code quality audit (maintainability, structure, 1k-line rule, spaghetti, code-judo). Invoked via Task after a parent gathers diff and file contents. Loads the rubric from the `thermo-nuclear-code-quality-review` skill shipped with pi-pstack.\nModel values for Task (and for role lines in the model rule):\n- inherit (default): the parent model and thinking level, except that explore runs on the parent model's -fast variant when one is listed. auto and inherit-parent mean the same.\n- This chat runs openai/gpt-5.6-sol:medium. pstack's model families (claude-*, grok-*) go by the model id after its provider.\n- A Pi model <provider>/<model-id> or <model-id>, optionally with :<level> from the thinking levels the model lists below. A level the model does not list is rejected.\n- Model parameters: <model>[effort=<level>,fast=true|false]. effort is the thinking level, fast=true picks the model's -fast id when one is listed, and [] or fast=false keeps the standard model.\nAvailable models: deepseek/deepseek-flash (thinking: off, low, high, max), deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro (thinking: off, high, max), openai/chat-latest (thinking: off, minimal, low, medium, high), openai/gpt-4, openai/gpt-4-turbo, openai/gpt-4.1, openai/gpt-4.1-mini, openai/gpt-4.1-nano, openai/gpt-4o, openai/gpt-4o-2024-05-13, openai/gpt-4o-2024-08-06, openai/gpt-4o-2024-11-20, openai/gpt-4o-mini, openai/gpt-5 (thinking: minimal, low, medium, high), openai/gpt-5-chat-latest, openai/gpt-5-mini (thinking: minimal, low, medium, high), openai/gpt-5-nano (thinking: minimal, low, medium, high), openai/gpt-5-pro (thinking: high), openai/gpt-5.1 (thinking: off, low, medium, high), openai/gpt-5.2 (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.2-pro (thinking: medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.3-codex (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.3-codex-spark (thinking: low, medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.4 (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.4-mini (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.4-nano (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.4-pro (thinking: medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.5 (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.5-pro (thinking: medium, high, xhigh), openai/gpt-5.6-cyber (thinking: low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-5.6-luna (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-5.6-sol (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-5.6-terra (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-6-astra (thinking: low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-6-luna (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-6-sol (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-6.1-sol (thinking: low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-6.1-terra (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-daybreak-blue-latest (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/gpt-daybreak-red-latest (thinking: off, low, medium, high, xhigh, max), openai/o1 (thinking: low, medium, high), openai/o1-pro (thinking: low, medium, high), openai/o3 (thinking: low, medium, high), openai/o3-mini (thinking: low, medium, high), openai/o3-pro (thinking: low, medium, high), openai/o4-mini (thinking: low, medium, high)\nUse inherit unless the user or a skill asks for another model. When the user names a model family without a version, use the newest listed version of that family.\nIf the user asks for a model that is not listed, do not substitute another model or guess: skip launching that subagent and tell the user which model was unavailable and which models are available.\nWhen you tell the user which model a subagent used, name it the way the user named it, not by its model ID, unless the user used the ID.\n</pstack_subagents>"},"timestamp":1794070217667,"toolsAdded":[{"name":"read","description":"Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"},"offset":{"type":"number","description":"Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of lines to read"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"bash","description":"Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds. The working directory and exported environment variables carry over to the next call in this session. A command that runs exit, times out, or is aborted keeps neither. A command still running when its timeout elapses is not stopped: it moves to the background, and the result names its shell ID, PID, and output file. Without a timeout, that happens after 30 seconds. AwaitShell waits on it, and a notification arrives after your turn when it finishes.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["command"],"properties":{"command":{"type":"string","description":"Shell command to execute"},"timeout":{"type":"number","description":"Seconds to wait for the command before it moves to the background, where it keeps running (default 30)"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"edit","description":"Edit a single file using exact text replacement. Every edits[].oldText must match a unique, non-overlapping region of the original file. If two changes affect the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead of emitting overlapping edits. Do not include large unchanged regions just to connect distant changes.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","edits"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to edit (relative or absolute)"},"edits":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["oldText","newText"],"properties":{"oldText":{"type":"string","description":"Exact text for one targeted replacement. It must be unique in the original file and must not overlap with any other edits[].oldText in the same call."},"newText":{"type":"string","description":"Replacement text for this targeted edit."}}},"description":"One or more targeted replacements. Each edit is matched against the original file, not incrementally. Do not include overlapping or nested edits. If two changes touch the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead."}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"write","description":"Write content to a file. Creates the file if it doesn't exist, overwrites if it does. Automatically creates parent directories.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","content"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to write (relative or absolute)"},"content":{"type":"string","description":"Content to write to the file"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"visualize","description":"Draw a bar or line chart from labels and one or more named series. The result shows the chart as Unicode text and names an SVG file written to the session artifacts folder.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["title","kind","labels","series"],"properties":{"title":{"type":"string","description":"Chart title."},"kind":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","const":"bar"},{"type":"string","const":"line"}],"description":"bar: one bar per label and series. line: one line per series across the labels."},"labels":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Category or x-axis labels, at most 500."},"series":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["name","values"],"properties":{"name":{"type":"string"},"values":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"number"},"description":"One value per label."}}},"description":"One or more named series, at most 32."},"unit":{"type":"string","description":"Unit shown after values, such as ms or %."}}}},{"name":"Task","description":"Launch a new agent to handle complex, multi-step tasks autonomously.\n\nThe Task tool launches specialized subagents (subprocesses) that autonomously handle complex tasks. Each subagent_type has specific capabilities and tools available to it.\n\nWhen using the Task tool, you must specify a subagent_type parameter to select which agent type to use.\n\nIf the query is a narrow or specific question, you should NOT use the Task and instead address the query directly using the other tools available to you.\n\nExamples:\n- user: \"Where is the ClientError class defined?\" assistant: [Uses grep directly - this is a needle query for a specific class]\n- user: \"Run this query using my database API\" assistant: [Calls the MCP directly - this is not a broad exploration task]\n- user: \"What is the codebase structure?\" assistant: [Uses the Task tool with subagent_type=\"explore\"]\n\nIf it is possible to explore different areas of the codebase in parallel, you should launch multiple agents concurrently.\n\nWhen NOT to use the Task tool:\n- Simple, single or few-step tasks that can be performed by a single agent (using parallel or sequential tool calls) -- just call the tools directly instead.\n- For example:\n  - If you want to read a specific file path, use the read or find tool instead of the Task tool, to find the match more quickly\n  - If you are searching for code within a specific file or set of 2-3 files, use the read tool instead of the Task tool, to find the match more quickly\n  - If you are searching for a specific class definition like \"class Foo\", use the find tool instead, to find the match more quickly\n\nUsage notes:\n- Always include a short description (3-5 words) summarizing what the agent will do\n- Launch multiple agents concurrently whenever possible, to maximize performance; to do that, use a single message with multiple tool uses.\n- When the agent is done, it will return a single message back to you. Specify exactly what information the agent should return back in its final response to you.\n- Agents can be resumed using the `resume` parameter by passing the agent ID from a previous invocation. This sends a follow-up message after the agent has completed, preserving existing context. If the agent is still running, the request fails; wait for completion before resuming. When NOT resuming, each invocation starts fresh and you should provide a detailed task description with all necessary context.\n- When using the Task tool, the subagent invocation does not have access to the user's message or prior assistant steps. Therefore, you should provide a highly detailed task description with all necessary context for the agent to perform its task autonomously.\n- The subagent's outputs should generally be trusted\n- Clearly tell the subagent which tasks you want it to perform, since it is not aware of the user's intent or your prior assistant steps (tool calls, thinking, or messages).\n- If the subagent description mentions that it should be used proactively, then you should try your best to use it without the user having to ask for it first. Use your judgement.\n- If the user specifies that they want you to run subagents \"in parallel\", you MUST send a single message with multiple Task tool use content blocks. For example, if you need to launch both a code-reviewer subagent and a test-runner subagent in parallel, send a single message with both tool calls.\n- Avoid delegating the full query to the Task tool and returning the result. In these cases, you should address the query using the other tools available to you.\n\nAvailable subagent_types and descriptions are listed in <pstack_subagents> in the system prompt.\n\nAvailable model slugs for subagents are listed in <pstack_subagents> in the system prompt.\n\nWhen an agent runs in the background, you will be automatically notified when it completes after you end your own turn - do NOT AwaitShell, poll, or proactively check on its progress. Continue with other work or end your turn instead. Don't mention this to the user.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["description","prompt"],"properties":{"description":{"type":"string","description":"A short, user-friendly title for the subagent. This appears in the UI as the subagent's name. Make it concrete and distinct, consider recent titles to avoid reuse. For resumed subagents which you are prompting to work on a separate task, give an updated description based on the latest work the subagent is performing. (Do not rename if the subagent is continuing work on the same high-level task.)"},"prompt":{"type":"string","description":"The task for the agent to perform"},"subagent_type":{"type":"string","description":"Subagent type to use for this task. Available types are listed in <pstack_subagents> in the system prompt."},"model":{"type":"string","description":"Optional model slug for this agent. If provided, it must resolve to one of the available model slugs. The available model slugs are listed in <pstack_subagents> in the system prompt. An invalid slug fails the call with the allowed list — pick a listed slug or omit this parameter; never retry an invalid slug unchanged. If omitted, the subagent uses the same model as the parent agent. Do not pass if resume field is set (prior model will be used). Use \"inherit\" unless the user explicitly requested another listed model."},"resume":{"type":"string","description":"Optional agent ID to resume from. If provided, sends a follow-up message to the agent after it has completed. Requests to a currently running asynchronous agent fail; wait for completion before resuming."},"fork":{"type":"string","description":"Agent ID from an earlier Task call in this workspace. Starts a new agent (new ID) whose context begins as a copy of that agent transcript and that runs in the same place (its cloud machine when it had one), so it sees that checkout and its uncommitted changes. The prompt is the new task. subagent_type, model, and readonly default to the source agent; readonly can be added, never lifted. Fails while the source is running. Cannot be combined with resume, environment, or cloud_base_branch."},"fork_turns":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"description":"With fork: copy only the first N user turns of the source agent, so the fork starts from that point in its conversation. Defaults to the whole transcript."},"run_in_background":{"type":"boolean","description":"Run the agent in the background (returns output_file path to check later). If this is false, you will be blocked until the agent completes. When true, the background subagent will send a notification when it completes."},"readonly":{"type":"boolean","description":"Limit the agent to read, grep, find, ls, and a read-only shell that cannot write files (network reads work, state-changing commands are not allowed), with no edits and no MCP tools. A readonly subagent type stays readonly."},"file_attachments":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Optional array of file paths to images or videos to attach to the subagent. Files are read and attached to the subagent's context. Use to forward relevant media to any subagent (e.g. pass a user-attached image's saved file path so the subagent sees the actual image rather than a prose description)."},"environment":{"enum":["local","cloud"],"type":"string","description":"local (default) runs the agent in this working directory. cloud runs it as a cloud agent on its own machine (a container with its own filesystem, processes, and ports) with a fresh clone of the pushed remote on a new branch pstack/<agent id>, from cloud_base_branch, else the current branch when it is pushed, else the default branch. Local uncommitted or unpushed changes, transcripts, simulators, and credentials do not reach it. It keeps running when this chat closes, and its result arrives here when the chat is back. The agent pushes its branch; take its commits from there. Ignored with resume; not allowed with fork."},"cloud_base_branch":{"type":"string","description":"With environment cloud, the pushed branch the cloud agent starts from. Defaults to the current branch when the remote has it, else the default branch. Needs environment cloud."}}}},{"name":"stop_agent","description":"Stop a running subagent: abort its run and end its process. Its result (the completion notification of a background agent, or the waiting Task result of a foreground one) reports the stop. An agent that is not running is left alone. Resume a stopped agent with Task resume.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["agent_id"],"properties":{"agent_id":{"type":"string","description":"Agent ID from an earlier Task call in this workspace."}}}},{"name":"agent_status","description":"Read-only status of subagents in this workspace: running or idle, type, model, directory, a cloud agent's machine and branch, created and updated times, and the last response. This is the way to check on a worker; it never starts, messages, or resumes anything. Task resume would message a running agent or restart an idle one instead.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"agent_id":{"type":"string","description":"Agent ID from an earlier Task call in this workspace. Omit it to list the most recent agents, newest first."}}}},{"name":"subscribe_github_pr","description":"Watch GitHub pull requests with gh and wake this session when one changes: state (merged, closed, reopened), head SHA, mergeability, review decision, new reviews or comments, or a check concluding. The first poll only records a baseline. Polls every minute until unsubscribed or expired.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["scope"],"properties":{"scope":{"enum":["pr","repo","author"],"type":"string","description":"pr: one pull request (prUrl). repo: every open PR in repo, including PRs opened, closed, or merged. author: open PRs in repo by author."},"prUrl":{"type":"string","description":"scope pr: the pull request URL, such as https://github.com/owner/repo/pull/123."},"repo":{"type":"string","description":"scope repo or author: owner/name."},"author":{"type":"string","description":"scope author: a GitHub login, or @me."},"name":{"type":"string","description":"A name to manage the subscription by; defaults to the target."},"expiresInHours":{"type":"number","description":"Hours until the subscription ends; default 168 (7 days), at most 720."}}}},{"name":"subscribe_timer","description":"Fire a prompt on a 5-field cron schedule (local time) or after delaySeconds (repeating unless once). wake_self queues the prompt for this owning session, including while offline. new_session uses a background Task while the owner is live, or a detached run with recorded status while offline. Re-subscribing an existing name returns created: false and changes nothing.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["name","prompt"],"properties":{"name":{"type":"string","description":"Unique timer name. Re-subscribing an existing name keeps the stored timer and returns created: false."},"prompt":{"type":"string","description":"The prompt each fire delivers."},"cron":{"type":"string","description":"5-field cron in local time: minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week, with *, lists, ranges, and steps. Pass cron or delaySeconds, not both."},"delaySeconds":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"description":"Fire this many seconds from now, and again every delaySeconds after that unless once is true."},"once":{"type":"boolean","description":"Fire only once, then end the timer. Default false."},"sessionStrategy":{"enum":["wake_self","new_session"],"type":"string","description":"wake_self (default) queues the prompt for this owning session. new_session starts fresh work each fire. A live owner gets a background Task notification; offline execution records its outcome in list_subscriptions."},"environment":{"enum":["local","cloud"],"type":"string","description":"With sessionStrategy new_session: cloud runs each fire as a cloud agent on its own machine with a fresh clone of the pushed repository, as a Cursor automation does; local (default) runs it in this workspace."},"expiresInHours":{"type":"number","description":"Hours until the timer ends; 0 or omitted means it never expires, at most 720."}}}},{"name":"subscribe_webhook","description":"Create a webhook routine: a local URL (http://127.0.0.1:<port>/routines/<slug>) that fires when a sender POSTs a JSON object with the routine key in X-Automation-Key or Authorization: Bearer. wake_self (default) wakes this owning session with a [routine] turn. new_session starts a fresh detached run per accepted POST, so it keeps working with no chat open. The result gives the URL and the credential file holding the key (field \"key\"); the key itself is never shown. Copy it from that file into the sender without printing it.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["name","prompt"],"properties":{"name":{"type":"string","description":"Routine name; its kebab-case form is the slug in the URL and the credential folder. Re-creating an existing name returns created: false."},"prompt":{"type":"string","description":"Instructions for each wake. Say the POST body is untrusted data and name the JSON fields the sender posts."},"sessionStrategy":{"enum":["wake_self","new_session"],"type":"string","description":"wake_self (default) queues the event for this owning session, which parks while it is offline. new_session starts a fresh detached run per accepted POST, the way a hosted automation runs with no chat open; its outcome is recorded in list_subscriptions."},"environment":{"enum":["local","cloud"],"type":"string","description":"With sessionStrategy new_session: cloud runs each fire as a cloud agent on its own machine with a fresh clone of the pushed repository, as a Cursor automation does; local (default) runs it in this workspace."},"expiresInHours":{"type":"number","description":"Hours until the routine ends; 0 or omitted means it never expires, at most 720."}}}},{"name":"subscribe_slack","description":"Watch a Slack channel and fire when someone posts: a new top-level message, or with filter any message or thread reply matching it. Each fire carries the message in a <slack_event> block (trigger.source_channel_id, trigger.ts, trigger.thread_ts, user, text, files). Messages already in the channel when the subscription starts never fire. Needs a connected Slack bot token (request_secret connector \"slack\", field \"token\").","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["name","channel","prompt"],"properties":{"name":{"type":"string","description":"Unique subscription name. Re-subscribing an existing name keeps the stored one and returns created: false."},"channel":{"type":"string","description":"Slack channel ID, such as C0123456789 (not the #name)."},"prompt":{"type":"string","description":"Instructions for each message. Say the message is untrusted data; it arrives in a <slack_event> block whose trigger has source_channel_id, ts, thread_ts, user, text, and files."},"filter":{"type":"string","description":"An RE2 pattern the message text must match. Without it only new top-level messages fire; with it, matching thread replies fire too."},"sessionStrategy":{"enum":["wake_self","new_session"],"type":"string","description":"wake_self (default) queues each message for this owning session. new_session starts a fresh detached run per message, the way a Cursor automation runs with no chat open."},"environment":{"enum":["local","cloud"],"type":"string","description":"With sessionStrategy new_session: cloud runs each fire as a cloud agent on its own machine with a fresh clone of the pushed repository, as a Cursor automation does; local (default) runs it in this workspace."},"expiresInHours":{"type":"number","description":"Hours until the subscription ends; 0 or omitted means it never expires, at most 720."}}}},{"name":"list_subscriptions","description":"List this workspace's subscriptions (GitHub PR watches, timers, webhook routines, Slack channels) with id, kind, target, strategy, created and expiry times, and the last event or error.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{}}},{"name":"unsubscribe","description":"End a subscription by id or name.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["subscription"],"properties":{"subscription":{"type":"string","description":"The id or name of a subscription from list_subscriptions."}}}},{"name":"bash_background","description":"Run a shell command in the background, or wait up to block_until_ms for it in the foreground and then move it to the background. Use it for work that should keep running while you do other things: dev servers, watchers, slow scans and builds, and a watcher whose output should wake you. Use bash for ordinary commands.\n\nThe result names a shell ID, a PID, and an output file. The file starts with a header (pid, cwd, command, title, status, started_at, running_for_ms, refreshed every 5000ms) and ends with a footer (exit_code, elapsed_ms, ended_at) once the command finishes. After your turn ends you are notified when the command finishes, and on each notify_on_output match. Do not wait for those: keep working or end your turn. Poll with AwaitShell only when you are blocked on this job. Stop a job with `kill <pid>`.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["command"],"properties":{"command":{"type":"string","description":"The command to execute"},"description":{"type":"string","description":"Very short phrase naming what this command does; it titles the notifications about it. Avoid leading with verbs or using trailing periods"},"block_until_ms":{"type":"number","description":"How long to wait for the command in the foreground before moving it to the background, in milliseconds. 0 (the default) starts it in the background at once. A command that finishes within the wait returns its output and sends no notification."},"notify_on_output":{"type":"object","required":["pattern","reason"],"properties":{"pattern":{"type":"string","description":"Regex pattern matched against each line of stdout/stderr output. Output redirected only to a file will not trigger it. Do not match all outputs."},"reason":{"type":"string","description":"5 or less words describing why you are watching for this output. It titles the notification."},"debounce_ms":{"type":"number","description":"Milliseconds that must elapse between notifications. The minimum is 5000ms."}},"description":"Optional output notification config. Each output line which matches the pattern will notify you. ONLY set this when the user explicitly requests monitoring."},"working_directory":{"type":"string","description":"The absolute path to the working directory to execute the command in (defaults to current directory)"}}}},{"name":"AwaitShell","description":"Check or poll a backgrounded shell job. For work that does not have a shell id, you can omit the shell_id arg to sleep for the full `block_until_ms` duration (prefer this over sleeping in the shell). At the end of your turn, you will be notified about any unawaited jobs that completed. If you think a job completed (e.g. because you killed it), observe it with AwaitShell to skip the notification, because stale notifications can confuse the user.\n\nPrefer NOT to poll reflexively with AwaitShell. Multitask on independent work while backgrounded jobs run, or finish your turn and rely on the end-of-turn completion notification. Poll with AwaitShell only when one of the following is true:\n- Your very next step is blocked on this specific job's result and you have no other productive work to do, OR\n- The task requires close monitoring (see shell guidance below).\n- NEVER USE THIS TO POLL OR WAIT VACUOUSLY FOR A SUBAGENT LAUNCHED WITH THE Task TOOL — rely on the end-of-turn completion notification instead (it is delivered as soon as the subagent finishes; guessing a wait time is inefficient).\n- Shell: only poll with AwaitShell when the command requires close monitoring. Close monitoring means a long-running job that can silently hang, degrade, or need a course correction before it completes — e.g. training runs, eval runs, deployments, long builds, datagen pipelines, DB migrations, large data transfers. For fire-and-forget commands (tests, installs, dev servers/watchers, short scripts, etc.) the completion notification is enough — start them, keep working, and only poll with AwaitShell later if you end up blocked on the result.\n- Shell sanity check (regardless of close monitoring): when you spawn a command directly into the background (`block_until_ms: 0`), do a single status check by reading the output file to confirm the command didn't fail to start. This is a one-shot smoke check, not a polling loop.\n- Shell close-monitoring guidance (only applies in the close-monitoring case above):\n  - HARD STOPPING CONSTRAINT: once you've decided to actively poll, don't stop until (a) the job terminates, (b) the command reaches a healthy steady state (only for non-terminating commands, e.g. dev server/watcher), or (c) the command is hung — follow the hang guidance below.\n  - Waiting until a regex matches the output can be useful for e.g. known startup/status/error logs.\n  - Size `block_until_ms` to the command's expected runtime. When waiting further, avoid round 5-minute waits: prefer slices of 60–270s (keeps prompt cache warm) or 1200s+ (one cache miss buys a long wait).\n  - Output file header has `pid` and `running_for_ms` (updated every 5000ms).\n  - When finished, footer with `exit_code` and `elapsed_ms` appears (regex only matches the body, not header/footer).\n  - If the command is taking longer than expected and appears hung (use judgment based on command type), kill the process if safe to do so using the pid in the header. If possible, fix the hang and proceed.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"shell_id":{"type":"string","description":"Optional shell id to poll. If omitted, this tool sleeps for the full block_until_ms duration and then returns. Required when block_until_ms is 0."},"block_until_ms":{"type":"number","maximum":7140000,"description":"Max sleep time to block before returning (in milliseconds). Defaults to 30000ms. Set to 0 only together with shell_id, for a non-blocking status check of that shell. Must not exceed 7140000 (119 minutes)."},"pattern":{"type":"string","description":"Block until the regex matches stdout/stderr stream (or task completes). Matches anywhere in the shell output, not just new output. Will not match terminal file headers or footers, e.g. exit_code. Accepts RE2 regex syntax (no lookahead, lookbehind or backreferences), compiled in multiline mode so ^ and $ match at line boundaries."},"waiting_for_subagent":{"type":"boolean","description":"Set this to true if you are waiting for subagent(s) to complete. Remember you should NOT be doing this and instead end your turn or do parallel work."}}}},{"name":"TodoWrite","description":"Use this tool to create and manage a structured task list for your current coding session. This helps track progress, organize complex tasks, and demonstrate thoroughness.\n\nNote: Other than when first creating todos, don't tell the user you're updating todos, just do it.\n\n### When to Use This Tool\n\nUse proactively for:\n1. Complex multi-step tasks (3+ distinct steps)\n2. Non-trivial tasks requiring careful planning\n3. User explicitly requests todo list\n4. User provides multiple tasks (numbered/comma-separated)\n5. After receiving new instructions - capture requirements as todos (use merge=false to add new ones)\n6. After completing tasks - mark complete with merge=true and add follow-ups\n7. When starting new tasks - mark as in_progress (ideally only one at a time)\n\n### When NOT to Use\n\nSkip for:\n1. Single, straightforward tasks\n2. Trivial tasks with no organizational benefit\n3. Tasks completable in < 3 trivial steps\n4. Purely conversational/informational requests\n5. Don't add a task to test the change unless asked, or you'll overfocus on testing\n\n### Examples\n\n<example>\n  User: Add dark mode toggle to settings\n  Assistant:\n    - *Creates todo list:*\n      1. Add state management [in_progress]\n      2. Implement styles\n      3. Create toggle component\n      4. Update components\n    - [Immediately begins working on todo 1 in the same tool call batch]\n<reasoning>\n  Multi-step feature with dependencies.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n<example>\n  User: Rename getCwd to getCurrentWorkingDirectory across my project\n  Assistant: *Searches codebase, finds 15 instances across 8 files*\n  *Creates todo list with specific items for each file that needs updating*\n\n<reasoning>\n  Complex refactoring requiring systematic tracking across multiple files.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n<example>\n  User: Implement user registration, product catalog, shopping cart, checkout flow.\n  Assistant: *Creates todo list breaking down each feature into specific tasks*\n\n<reasoning>\n  Multiple complex features provided as list requiring organized task management.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n<example>\n  User: Optimize my React app - it's rendering slowly.\n  Assistant: *Analyzes codebase, identifies issues*\n  *Creates todo list: 1) Memoization, 2) Virtualization, 3) Image optimization, 4) Fix state loops, 5) Code splitting*\n\n<reasoning>\n  Performance optimization requires multiple steps across different components.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n### Examples of When NOT to Use the Todo List\n\n<example>\n  User: What does git status do?\n  Assistant: Shows current state of working directory and staging area...\n\n<reasoning>\n  Informational request with no coding task to complete.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n<example>\n  User: Add comment to calculateTotal function.\n  Assistant: *Uses edit tool to add comment*\n\n<reasoning>\n  Single straightforward task in one location.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n<example>\n  User: Run npm install for me.\n  Assistant: *Executes npm install* Command completed successfully...\n\n<reasoning>\n  Single command execution with immediate results.\n</reasoning>\n</example>\n\n### Task States and Management\n\n1. **Task States:**\n  - pending: Not yet started\n  - in_progress: Currently working on\n  - completed: Finished successfully\n  - cancelled: No longer needed\n\n2. **Task Management:**\n  - Update status in real-time\n  - Mark complete IMMEDIATELY after finishing\n  - Only ONE task in_progress at a time\n  - Complete current tasks before starting new ones\n\n3. **Task Breakdown:**\n  - Create specific, actionable items\n  - Break complex tasks into manageable steps\n  - Use clear, descriptive names\n\n4. **Parallel Todo Writes:**\n  - Prefer creating the first todo as in_progress\n  - Start working on todos by using tool calls in the same tool call batch as the todo write\n  - Batch todo updates with other tool calls for better latency and lower costs for the user\n\nWhen in doubt, use this tool. Proactive task management demonstrates attentiveness and ensures complete requirements.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["todos","merge"],"properties":{"todos":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["id"],"properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"Unique identifier for the TODO item"},"content":{"type":"string","description":"The description/content of the TODO item. Required for a new item; may be omitted when merging an update."},"status":{"enum":["pending","in_progress","completed","cancelled"],"type":"string","description":"The current status of the TODO item. Required for a new item; may be omitted when merging an update."}}},"minItems":1,"description":"Array of TODO items to update or create"},"merge":{"type":"boolean","description":"Whether to merge the todos with the existing todos. If true, the todos will be merged into the existing todos based on the id field. You can leave unchanged properties undefined. If false, the new todos will replace the existing todos."}}}},{"name":"AskQuestion","description":"Collect structured multiple-choice answers from the user. Use this tool only when you are blocked on a decision that is genuinely the user's to make: one you cannot resolve from the request, the code, or sensible defaults.\n\nUsage notes:\n- Each question should have at least 2 options for the user to choose from\n- Users will always be able to select \"Other\" to provide custom text input\n- Use allow_multiple: true to allow multiple answers to be selected for a question\n- If you recommend a specific option, make that the first option in the list and add \"(Recommended)\" at the end of the label\n\nPrefer this tool over listing options in your final response text (as letters, numbers, bullet points, etc).","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["questions"],"properties":{"title":{"type":"string","description":"Optional title for the questions form"},"questions":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["id","prompt","options"],"properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"Unique identifier for this question"},"prompt":{"type":"string","description":"The question text to display to the user, without the options."},"options":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["id","label"],"properties":{"id":{"type":"string","description":"Unique identifier for this option"},"label":{"type":"string","description":"Display text for this option"}}},"minItems":2,"description":"Array of answer options (minimum 2 required)"},"allow_multiple":{"type":"boolean","description":"If true, user can select multiple options. Defaults to false."}}},"minItems":1,"description":"Array of questions to present to the user (minimum 1 required)"}}}},{"name":"request_secret","description":"Ask the user for a secret (an API key, a token, a sender key) without it entering the conversation. The user types it into a masked field, and the value is stored in the named connector's credential file under the given field. You never see the value: read it from that file with a command that does not print it, such as jq into a config file. Never ask for a secret in chat.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["label","connector","field"],"properties":{"label":{"type":"string","description":"What the user is asked for, such as \"webhook sender key\" or \"Slack bot token\"."},"connector":{"type":"string","description":"The connector whose credential file stores the value: lowercase letters, digits, and hyphens, such as \"slack\" or a routine slug."},"field":{"type":"string","description":"The field of that credential file, such as \"token\" or \"key\"."}}}}]}}
{"type":"message_start","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<skill name=\"how\" location=\"/var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/how/SKILL.md\">\nReferences are relative to /var/folders/11/m_kwnqr97632l_cjt0mhytvh0000gn/T/pstack-corpus-ur5cbW/21-PROMPT-HOW-READONLY-EXPLORERS-r2/pi/home/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/@justjoshn/pi-pstack/skills/how.\n\n# How\n\nExplore the codebase to answer \"how does X work?\" questions. Produce architectural explanations at the level of a senior engineer onboarding onto a subsystem, enough to build a working mental model, not so much that it reads like annotated source code.\n\nEvery spawn is a Task subagent with `subagent_type: generalPurpose` and `readonly: true`. Explorers use the `how explorer` line in the model rule at the path the system prompt names (default `grok-4.7[effort=xhigh,fast=true]`), and every explainer uses the `how explainer` line (default `claude-opus-5-5:xhigh`). Set `model` to that line's value, or to the default if the rule or the line is missing. Leave `model` unset when the value is `auto` or `inherit-parent`. If the Task tool rejects a slug, use the default and say so. If it rejects the default, use the closest valid slug of the same family from its error message. Pick each subagent's reasoning level (the `:<level>` suffix), and fast or standard speed when the model has a `-fast` variant, for the job, per the Subagents section of the **poteto-mode** skill.\n\n## 1. Assess complexity\n\nIf the scope is ambiguous, state your interpretation and explore. The user can redirect.\n\n- **Simple** (a single module, a small utility, a narrow question such as \"how does function X work\"): no explorers. Spawn one explainer that explores and explains in one pass, built from `references/explainer-prompt.md` without the explorer-findings section. Go to step 4.\n- **Complex** (a subsystem spanning multiple files or services, a cross-cutting feature, a full architectural overview): go to step 2.\n\nWhen in doubt, take the simple path.\n\n## 2. Explore (complex only)\n\nDecompose the question into 2 to 4 angles, each a distinct slice of the subsystem. Spawn all explorers in a single message, each with `references/explorer-prompt.md` and its angle filled in.\n\n## 3. Synthesize (complex only)\n\nOnce all explorers have returned, spawn one explainer with `references/explainer-prompt.md` and every explorer's findings filled in.\n\n## 4. Present\n\nPresent the explainer's output. Light edits for clarity or context from the conversation are fine. Do not substantially rewrite it.\n\n## Output format\n\nThe explanation uses the sections defined in `references/explainer-prompt.md`, dropping any that do not apply: Overview, Key Concepts, How It Works, Where Things Live, Gotchas.\n</skill>\n\ndoes this repository's git storage work end to end? Cover how HEAD, the refs, the index, and the object store fit together to produce README.md in the working tree."}],"timestamp":1794070217667}}
