{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_end","contentIndex":1,"toolCall":{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_3d26c65ad3334d30a2cda2cde7e9b2f3|fc_0b3ed17c6d7d26f6016aef57830d9887d09f1809978398bbc6","name":"Task","arguments":{"description":"Synthesize git storage flow","prompt":"You are writing an architectural explanation for a senior engineer. Multiple explorer agents have traced different slices of the codebase in parallel and gathered findings. Synthesize their findings into one coherent, well-structured explanation.\n\n## Original Question\n\n> Does this repository's git storage work end to end? Cover how HEAD, the refs, the index, and the object store fit together to produce README.md in the working tree.\n\n## Explorer Findings\n\nExplorer 1 (HEAD/refs) found symbolic HEAD -> refs/heads/main -> commit d9722409cabd904e7339fda14fe1818d77be6641, with no packed-refs and all objects loose. It reported commit -> tree 8c1828... -> blob 7e4764..., but this conflicts with the other two explorers and likely reverses tree/blob IDs. It found the working file has 34 bytes and the index is v2 with one entry.\n\nExplorer 2 (index) decoded .git/index as DIRC v2, one stage-0 README.md entry, mode 100644, size 34, blob 8c1828de80f220d2d9d841f6d6380c29e1a9bc54. The TREE extension caches root tree 7e476488da437541819d424ac7276cf48b3491a6. It found a structurally present 20-byte checksum trailer. It traced HEAD commit d9722409... -> tree 7e476488... -> README blob 8c1828de..., matching index and working file.\n\nExplorer 3 (object store) independently found commit d9722409... at .git/objects/d9/..., root tree 7e476488... at .git/objects/7e/..., and README blob 8c1828de... at .git/objects/8c/.... The tree payload is 37 bytes: `100644 README.md\\0<20-byte blob id>`. The blob payload is 34 bytes: `# Unchanged prompt corpus fixture\\n`. All objects are loose; .git/objects/pack is empty. The index independently records blob 8c1828... and cached tree 7e4764....\n\nAll explorers report the command sandbox prevented running Git commands like git fsck/status/hash-object, so direct file/binary inspection was used. You have read-only access and should resolve the object-ID contradiction by checking repository files directly if possible. Be explicit about verification limits: distinguish direct structural evidence from unavailable command-level fsck/status verification.\n\n## Instructions\n\nThe explorers each investigated a different angle of the same subsystem. Their findings overlap and one has a contradiction. Reconcile them by checking code/storage yourself. Merge the slices into a unified picture.\n\nWrite an explanation a senior engineer unfamiliar with Git internals could read and walk away with a solid mental model, understanding architecture well enough to work with it confidently.\n\n## Output Format\n\nUse this structure, adapted as needed:\n\n### Overview\n1-2 paragraphs with a direct yes/no assessment.\n\n### Key Concepts\nBrief definitions of HEAD, refs, commit/tree/blob objects, index, working tree.\n\n### How It Works\nWalk concrete flow step by step with exact IDs and paths. Include a mermaid or ASCII diagram. Explain both committed-snapshot path and index/working-tree comparison path; clarify that checkout writes blobs to files but current artifacts prove consistency rather than recording historical checkout execution.\n\n### Where Things Live\nBrief path map.\n\n### Gotchas\nNon-obvious details and verification caveat.\n\nUse concrete language. Do not dump annotated source. Do not overclaim checks that could not run.","subagent_type":"generalPurpose","model":"claude-opus-5-5:xhigh","readonly":true,"run_in_background":false}}}}
