hes the same root tree.\n\n```text\nHEAD\n  → refs/heads/main\n  → commit d9722409…\n  → tree 7e476488…\n  → README.md, mode 100644\n  → blob 8c1828de…\n  → \"# Unchanged prompt corpus fixture\\n\"\n```\n\n### Key Concepts\n\n- **HEAD** identifies the current checkout. Here it is symbolic: it points to the `main` branch.\n- **Ref** is a named pointer, normally to a commit. `refs/heads/main` holds the current tip of `main`.\n- **Commit object** stores commit metadata and names a root tree.\n- **Tree object** represents a directory, mapping names and modes to blobs or nested trees.\n- **Blob object** stores raw file contents. It does not contain the filename.\n- **Index** is the proposed next snapshot—the staging area. It maps paths to object IDs and modes, plus cached filesystem metadata.\n- **Working tree** contains ordinary, mutable files such as `README.md`.\n\n### How It Works\n\n#### 1. `HEAD` selects the branch\n\n`.git/HEAD` contains:\n\n```text\nref: refs/heads/main\n```\n\nSo this is not a detached `HEAD`. Git follows the reference name to `.git/refs/heads/main`.\n\n#### 2. The branch selects the commit\n\n`.git/refs/heads/main` contains:\n\n```text\nd9722409cabd904e7339fda14fe1818d77be6641\n```\n\nThere is no `.git/packed-refs`, so `main` is stored entirely as a loose ref.\n\nThe corresponding loose commit object is:\n\n```text\n.git/objects/d9/722409cabd904e7339fda14fe1818d77be6641\n```\n\nGit splits a 40-character object ID after its first two characters to form the object path.\n\n#### 3. The commit selects the root tree\n\nThe commit names this root tree:\n\n```text\n7e476488da437541819d424ac7276cf48b3491a6\n```\n\nIts loose object is:\n\n```text\n.git/objects/7e/476488da437541819d424ac7276cf48b3491a6\n```\n\nThe tree contains one entry, conceptually:\n\n```text\n100644 README.md → 8c1828de80f220d2d9d841f6d6380c29e1a9bc54\n```\n\nThe actual tree payload stores the object ID as 20 binary bytes:\n\n```text\n100644 README.md\\0<20-byte blob ID>\n```\n\nThe 37-byte payload size checks out:\n\n- mode: 6 bytes\n- space: 1 byte\n- `README.md`: 9 bytes\n- NUL separator: 1 byte\n- binary SHA-1: 20 bytes\n\n#### 4. The blob supplies the file contents\n\nThe README blob is:\n\n```text\n8c1828de80f220d2d9d841f6d6380c29e1a9bc54\n```\n\nstored at:\n\n```text\n.git/objects/8c/1828de80f220d2d9d841f6d6380c29e1a9bc54\n```\n\nIts payload is 34 bytes:\n\n```text\n# Unchanged prompt corpus fixture\\n\n```\n\nThat matches the working-tree `README.md`, including its final newline.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    H[\".git/HEAD<br/>ref: refs/heads/main\"]\n    R[\".git/refs/heads/main<br/>d9722409…\"]\n    C[\"Commit d9722409…\"]\n    T[\"Root tree 7e476488…\"]\n    E[\"100644 README.md\"]\n    B[\"Blob 8c1828de…<br/>34 bytes\"]\n    W[\"Working-tree README.md\"]\n\n    H --> R\n    R --> C\n    C -->|tree field| T\n    T --> E\n    E --> B\n    B -. \"checkout materializes bytes\" .-> W\n```\n\nA checkout follows this commit-to-tree-to-blob graph and writes the blob bytes under the filename supplied by the tree. The present artifacts prove that the working file is consistent with such a checkout; they do not preserve evidence that a specific historical checkout command created it.\n\n#### 5. The index independently describes the staged snapshot\n\n`.git/index` is a version 2 `DIRC` index with one entry:\n\n| Field | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Path | `README.md` |\n| Stage | `0` |\n| Mode | `100644` |\n| Recorded size | 34 bytes |\n| Blob | `8c1828de80f220d2d9d841f6d6380c29e1a9bc54` |\n\nStage 0 means this is a normal, resolved entry. Stages 1–3 are used during unresolved merge conflicts.\n\nThe index agrees with the committed tree:\n\n```text\nHEAD tree's README.md blob = 8c1828de…\nIndex README.md blob       = 8c1828de…\n```\n\nTherefore, there is no staged content difference for this path.\n\nThe index also has a `TREE` cache extension containing:\n\n```text\n7e476488da437541819d424ac7276cf48b3491a6\n```\n\nThat is the same root tree named by the current commit. This extension is an optimization; Git can reconstruct the tree from the ordinary index entries.\n\n#### 6. The index connects to the working tree\n\nThe index stores both the expected blob ID and filesystem stat data such as size and timestamps.\n\nFor fast status checks, Git can initially use the stat data to determine whether `README.md` could have changed. When necessary, it reads the working file, frames it as a Git blob, hashes it, and compares the resulting object ID to the index entry.\n\nAll three views agree:\n\n```text\nCommitted snapshot: README.md → blob 8c1828de…\nIndex:             README.md → blob 8c1828de…\nWorking tree:      README.md = the blob's 34-byte payload\n```\n\n### Where Things Live\n\n| Purpose | Path |\n|---|---|\n| Current checkout pointer | `.git/HEAD` |\n| `main` branch ref | `.git/refs/heads/main` |\n| Commit object | `.git/objects/d9/722409cabd904e7339fda14fe1818d77be6641` |\n| Root tree object | `.git/objects/7e/476488da437541819d424ac7276cf48b3491a6` |\n| README blob object | `.git/objects/8c/1828de80f220d2d9d841f6d6380c29e1a9bc54` |\n| Staging index | `.git/index` |\n| Working file | `README.md` |\n| Pack directory | `.git/objects/pack/`—empty |\n| Packed refs | `.git/packed-refs`—absent |\n\nThis repository is small enough that its commit, tree, and blob are all loose objects rather than being stored in a packfile.\n\n### Gotchas\n\n- **The tree owns the filename.** The blob contains only bytes; it does not know it is called `README.md`.\n- **The index does not contain README’s contents.** It points to the blob in the object database.\n- **The index is not necessarily the same as either `HEAD` or the working tree.** In this repository they happen to agree.\n- **Git hashes a framed object.** The README object ID comes from `blob 34\\0` followed by the 34 content bytes—not from the content bytes alone.\n- **Tree and index object IDs are binary internally.** They are not stored as 40 printable hexadecimal characters.\n- **The index’s `TREE` extension is a cache**, not the authoritative source of individual staged paths.\n- **The correct IDs are:** root tree `7e476488…`, README blob `8c1828de…`.\n\nThe verification was storage-level: direct inspection established a coherent graph and matching contents. The environment prevented command-level checks such as `git fsck`, `git status`, and `git hash-object`, so the precise conclusion is: **the inspected storage is mutually consistent end to end, although Git’s full integrity checker was not run.**\n\n*The How skill’s configured Grok/Claude subagent defaults were unavailable in this environment, so the available parent model was used instead.*","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_0b3ed17c6d7d26f6016aef57f7792887d0879d704e9e15abf1\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"}],"api":"openai-responses","provider":"openai","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","usage":{"input":2446,"output":2030,"cacheRead":31872,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":108,"totalTokens":36348,"cost":{"input":0.009784,"output":0.040600000000000004,"cacheRead":0.012748800000000001,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0631328,"totalFromProvider":false}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1794070515753,"responseId":"resp_0b3ed17c6d7d26f6016aef57f45a8887d093f066691b397802","rawStopReason":"completed","durationMs":38210,"thinkingLevel":"medium"}}
